<!--
  reBot B601-RS + Gripette, bolted to the bench, with the CAN to pick up.

  The grasp counterpart of table.xml: same bench, same build-downward-from-z=0
  construction (see that file for why the table moves and the arm does not),
  with the red cube swapped for the can the OpenArm's grasp policies were
  trained on, and the contact settings a grasp needs.

  WHY THIS SCENE CAN RUN AN OPENARM CHECKPOINT
      The diffusion policies observe the wrist fisheye plus a 2-D gripper state
      and emit 11-D Cartesian deltas that SendCartesianDelta applies in the
      CAMERA-LOCAL frame. Nothing in that loop is expressed in the base frame.
      And the wrist assembly is the same hardware on both robots - measured
      from the two models, gripper_center sits at (3.5, 67.5, 87.5) mm in the
      camera frame on BOTH, same rotation. So a grasp looks the same through
      the lens here as it did on the OpenArm; what differs is the background
      and the arm's own links past the gripper.

  THE CAN is the one from openarm_gripette_simu/scenes/table_grasp.xml,
  unchanged: Ø50 x 120 mm, a single curved collision surface so it self-centers
  in the fingers instead of tumbling, silver end rims that are visual only. Its
  body/joint keep the `red_cube` names that scene uses - they are what the
  shared episode code looks up, and renaming them here would only make this
  scene the odd one out.

  PLACEMENT: x -0.60, chosen for THIS arm's kinematics rather than copied from
  the OpenArm's layout. The OpenArm is torso-mounted and human-like: shoulder
  above the work, arm hanging down, reaching a table 40 cm BELOW its base. The
  reBot is a desktop arm bolted to the bench - shoulder at the work surface,
  rest posture horizontal with the elbow folded - so the distance that suits it
  is not the same distance. Scoring the generator's start distribution against
  reBot IK, with the arm required to clear the bench (60 starts x 18
  arrangement yaws per position):

      can x   reach   clears 20mm   manipulability   shoulder_lift / elbow_flex
      -0.30     68%        37%            0.0018          87 deg /  22 deg
      -0.40     77%        43%            0.0028         102 deg /  22 deg
      -0.50     85%        47%            0.0050         119 deg /  56 deg
      -0.60     95%        52%            0.0111         134 deg /  78 deg
      -0.70    100%        57%            0.0146         152 deg / 109 deg

  At -0.30 the arm has to stand up and reach down at a can beneath itself - the
  torso-mounted posture, and the worst-conditioned pose in the range. -0.70
  scores best but lands on the slab's edge with nothing in reserve. -0.60 gives
  a leaning, folded-elbow posture, 6x the manipulability of -0.30, and still
  150 mm of bench beyond the can.

  BENCH EXTENT is part of the observation, not just scenery. The policy trained
  on a 300 x 300 mm table floating in a checkerboard void; this bench is what
  fills the lower half of its view. Narrowed to 360 mm across and ending 150 mm
  past the can so the frame holds a comparable patch of table against the same
  background. The arm end still reaches x +0.10 - it has to carry the arm.

  <option>/<default> are the OpenArm grasp scene's, for the same reason it has
  them: the default contact model lets a gripped cylinder slide out of the
  fingers. Elliptic friction cones with a high impratio and a few no-slip
  iterations are what make a held object stay held.
-->
<mujoco model="rebot_table_grasp">
    <option cone="elliptic" impratio="10" timestep="0.001" iterations="100" noslip_iterations="3" />
    <default>
        <geom friction="2.0 0.005 0.001" />
    </default>

    <include file="../../rebot_gripette_model/rebot_gripette_model/rebot_b601rs/robot.xml" />

    <visual>
        <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0" />
        <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1" />
        <global azimuth="160" elevation="-20" offwidth="1296" offheight="972" />
    </visual>

    <asset>
        <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512"
            height="3072" />
        <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4"
            rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8" width="300" height="300" />
        <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="5 5"
            reflectance="0.2" />

        <texture type="2d" name="wood" builtin="flat" rgb1="0.65 0.45 0.25" rgb2="0.55 0.35 0.18"
            mark="random" markrgb="0.60 0.40 0.22" width="256" height="256" />
        <material name="wood" texture="wood" texrepeat="3 3" shininess="0.1" specular="0.1" />

        <material name="red_cube" rgba="0.85 0.15 0.1 1" shininess="0.3" specular="0.5" />
        <material name="silver" rgba="0.6 0.6 0.63 1" shininess="0.6" specular="0.5"
            reflectance="0.2" />
    </asset>

    <worldbody>
        <light pos="-0.3 0 2.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true" />

        <!-- Floor, 73 cm below the table surface the arm is bolted to. -->
        <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" pos="0 0 -0.73" type="plane"
            material="groundplane" />

        <!-- The bench. Static geoms (no body joint): the table does not move,
             and the arm is bolted to it. The 10 cm slab THICKNESS matches
             rebot_b601rs/bench.xml, the bare model the real server measures
             its clearance gate against - same thickness, same verdicts. The
             EXTENT is this scene's own (see BENCH EXTENT above): x -0.75..0.10
             to end 150 mm past the can, y +/-0.18 so the frame holds about as
             much table as the training view did. -->
        <geom name="tabletop" type="box" size="0.425 0.18 0.05" pos="-0.325 0 -0.05"
            material="wood" />
        <geom name="leg_1" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.315" pos="0.06 0.14 -0.415"
            material="wood" />
        <geom name="leg_2" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.315" pos="-0.71 0.14 -0.415"
            material="wood" />
        <geom name="leg_3" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.315" pos="0.06 -0.14 -0.415"
            material="wood" />
        <geom name="leg_4" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.315" pos="-0.71 -0.14 -0.415"
            material="wood" />

        <!-- The can: Ø50 x 120 mm standing on the bench, so its centre is at
             half-height above z=0. -->
        <body name="red_cube" pos="-0.60 0 0.06">
            <freejoint name="red_cube_joint" />
            <geom name="red_cube" type="cylinder" size="0.025 0.06"
                material="red_cube" mass="0.1"
                friction="1.0 0.1 0.005" condim="6"
                solref="0.005 1" solimp="0.99 0.99 0.001" />
            <!-- Thin (~2 mm) silver end rims, radius 0.026 and centred ON the
                 red ends (z=±0.06) so nothing is coplanar - no z-fighting. -->
            <geom type="cylinder" size="0.026 0.001" pos="0 0 0.06"
                material="silver" contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" />
            <geom type="cylinder" size="0.026 0.001" pos="0 0 -0.06"
                material="silver" contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" />
        </body>
    </worldbody>
</mujoco>
